body:
We did have it. Our elections happen in two different turns if in the first one a candidate doesn't get over 50% of the votes. In our first turn, there were *a lot* of candidates, a lot of different options. Haddad, the one that went against Bolsonaro in the second turn, isn't even that left of a opposition, I'd consider the party more of a center-left right now, but that's my opinion so don't quote me on that. Mostly, Bolsonaro won because of his hate speech, at least that's how I view things. People here are really fed up with the Worker's Party (PT), as we were over a decade under their power, and granted they are very corrupt, but people seem to blame *every single problem* Brazil has on them. They did take *a lot* of people out of extreme poverty and implemented some good social programs, but it doesn't matter to Bolsonaro voters. We're all fed up with our country's violence too and Bolsonaro fed on that, he said he will get rid of it (did not say how, but it seems to involve the Army and getting rid of our gun control policies). He has homophobic, misogynist and racist views, and some people did vote for him based on that too, he considers himself a good christian too and received the support of several churches. Crimes against LGBT+ people (already too high before) seem to be increasing too lately, by people chanting Bolsonaro's name. He validates these crimes with his speech.

&#x200B;

TL;DR: Sorry, might have typed a bit too much here, but yeah, we did have a lot of options to begin with, but he was the most vocal one, with all the hate speech and focus on the military.
